<!--Post: 5585--><!--Time: 1107207878-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5585"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Verm_inve<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Verm_inve<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 31, 2005 - 1:44 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->There was a gentleman at my college that gave a presentation on Highway Art.  He was a phoptographer that would travel the countryside looking at places where a makeshift &#34;memorial&#34; was kept for victims of drunk driving accidents. <BR> <BR>What would be left there are homemade memorials of pictures or ribbons.  I don&#39;t know how popular this style of &#34;remembering the dead&#34; is in Kansas, but perhaps the BTK used ribbons on a ceral box to make it look like some memorial on the side of the road. <BR> <BR>That could explain the reason why no one thought it odd for these boxes to be left there ... and why no one inspected them.   <BR> <BR>My question is this:  Were there any previous driving accidents on this road?  Could BTK perhaps be making a statement on them?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5585-->
<!--Post: 5641--><!--Time: 1107230063-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5641"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->George<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->George<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Monday, January 31, 2005 - 7:54 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Verm_inve: I don&#39;t know the area or road but your mention of ribbons makes me wonder. If I&#39;m driving along and see a box with ribbons, I <u>may well</u> consider it a sad memorial to an accident victim. Point being, NO WAY would I  pull over for a closer look. Might BTK have put ribbons around the box to keep people away; lessen the chance that someone he didn&#39;t want picking up the box doing just that? That&#39;s a stretch but it seems the sort of thing BTK might do - and the double &#34;pun&#34; of auto accident victims, plus items or information relating to homicide all being in one little box seems to fit BTK&#39;s arrested sense of humor. <BR> <BR>One more. Due to 9/11, NO WAY am I picking up anything like the box. Could be a bomb or the container may be coated with ricin or anthrax. Unlikely and I may not think of it at the time but you never know. <BR> <BR>The info you seek may be available by checking with the county in which the box was placed, using the name of the road and auto fatalities. But that&#39;s a wild guess; it&#39;s possible such information is available online.  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5641-->
<!--Post: 5655--><!--Time: 1107253238-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5655"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Nugzter<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Nugzter<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 01, 2005 - 2:20 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->maybe its his way of putting his victims to rest, symbolicaly. a ritual of sorts.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5655-->
<!--Post: 5669--><!--Time: 1107275520-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5669"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Teardrop<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Teardrop<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->7-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, February 01, 2005 - 8:32 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->That practice is common in Texas and has actually caused the State to develop guidelines, as so many people have caused wrecks by trying to read them while driving or having stopped. It is also common to place balloons/streamers along roads to mark the routes and turns guiding people to parties. I think that he did not want the box to be mistaken for trash; hence the streamers. God forbid that BTK is a party planner! Though given his penchant for foresight, he would be a good one. Funeral directing would utilize his gothic creativity.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5669-->
<!--Post: 5817--><!--Time: 1107390850-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST5817"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Lmuncy316<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Lmuncy316<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Wednesday, February 02, 2005 - 4:34 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->People also mark places of death with decorations in the area where I live.  In addition to the balloons and ribbons I say the most redundant thing I see are decorated crucifixes.  Now, someone posted a message in the &#34;Messages from Btk&#34; thread as &#34;Cross of Christ&#34; &#40;or something like that&#41;.  I&#39;m not saying these incidents are related I&#39;m just wondering &#34;what if&#34;.  Hey, does anyone know if the road sign signaled anything to do with a nearby Crossroads or railroad crossing, Tom?<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 5817-->
